  "updated": "2026-08-02T06:48:08.282371+00:00",
  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nA warm front continues to push towards the forecast area this\nmorning. To the north some marine fog has lingered thru the day\nand is now pushing towards the Maine coastline. I anticipate it\nwill creep into the lower Kennebec Valley by early morning.\nAfter sunrise some scattering out of the stratus is expected,\nbut it should more or less hang out offshore all day.\n\nToday I also cannot rule out some showers or thunderstorms, but\nthe bulk of precip looks like it will hold off even thru the\novernight. So fog moves in again towards sunset and will be more\nwidespread tonight.\n\nThen showers and thunderstorms approach the region and try to\nmove thru the area early Mon. Model guidance is all over the\nplace with the heaviest rainfall, but the highest probabilities\nfor something greater than a half inch of QPF is over the\nsouthern half of NH. In addition the ECWMF EFI has continued to\nget more robust with rainfall thru the end of Mon. This includes\nmost of NH with 80 percent or more of the ensemble members with\na seasonably heavy rainfall. I believe that the best chances for\nsome appreciable rainfall will be over far southern NH as well\nas the North Country, both of which may see some convection\nbefore the frontal precip on Mon. The least likely spot for\nsignificant rainfall at this time looks like it will be west\ncentral Maine. Overall the NBM is generally below 1 inch QPF,\nwith most areas closer to a half inch or less. That seams\nreasonable than some of the more bullish recent rainfall events.\n\nWe will also have to keep an eye out for some stronger storms\nMon. With the slowing down of the system overall, the frontal\npassage is more favorably aligned with peak heating. As a result\nsome long range CAM guidance is now initiation quite a bit of\nthunderstorm activity along the boundary. Machine learning\nguidance shows the possibility of severe weather, and SPC has \nadded a portion of the forecast area to a marginal risk for \nsevere thunderstorms. Given the low confidence in heating with \nplenty of fog and low cloud cover possible, I do not see any \nreason to include enhanced wording at this time. However NBM \nthunder probabilities in the scattered range seem on target.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nSome showers may linger Monday night as the upper trough and \ncold front clear the area. Abundant clouds are going to bring \nabout another night with temperatures only managing to drop into\nthe low to mid 60s. By Tuesday we start to feel the influence \nof the Bermuda high once again as we end up dry with \ntemperatures in the low to mid 80s. Ridging aloft continues into\nWednesday, but the ridge axis moves east of the area kicking \noff an extended period of southwest flow. This will bring about \nhumid conditions as it continues through at least Saturday. Heat\nbuilds somewhat as well, but with the ridge mainly off to the \neast it won't be quite as dramatic as this time last month. We \nlikely see temperatures approach the 90F degree mark by the end\nof the week with the elevated humidity making it feel a bit \nwarmer, but nothing suggests major heat.\n\nAs far as precipitation chances, it looks like we are going to \nbe relying on diurnal instability and the potential for frontal \nboundaries to be stalled in the vicinity and/or shortwaves \nmoving through the broader trough to our west. Either way, \nwidespread rainfall is not looking likely and many locations may\ncontinue their dry streaks.",
